## What the code does

Three `_set` methods in calendars-client use the `ref` binding-mode pattern inside an `if let Some(...)` to avoid moving the optional create map:

```rust
// crate-jmap-calendars-client/src/methods/calendar.rs:96
if let Some(ref m) = create {
    for k in m.keys() { ... }
}
// ... later in the same function:
if let Some(c) = create {
    args["create"] = serde_json::to_value(&c).map_err(...)?;
}
```

The `ref` binding-mode is the older Rust spelling for "bind `m` as `&T` instead of moving `T`". The modern (since edition 2018) idiom is to take a reference to the scrutinee:

```rust
if let Some(m) = &create {
    for k in m.keys() { ... }
}
```

Same shape in event.rs:105, participant_identity.rs:79.

## What idiomatic Rust would do

`if let Some(m) = &create` is the standard 2018+ form. It reads left-to-right as "borrow create, match Some, bind contents as m". The `ref` form ("bind as borrowed") is a relic of pre-NLL Rust when the compiler couldn't always infer the binding mode from the scrutinee. Modern style guides and the standard library both prefer `&pattern`.

This is the kind of thing rust-analyzer or rustfmt-style tools will eventually rewrite automatically. Clippy itself doesn't flag it — `clippy::redundant_pattern_matching` and `clippy::needless_borrow` are about different patterns — but it's covered under the "language idioms" subsection of the review prompt explicitly as drive-by readability rot.

## Why it matters

Low stakes individually. The argument for filing is the canonical-template propagation rule: this code is about to (potentially) be copy-pasted into six more sibling crates if the empty-creation-id validation pattern propagates. Settling the idiomatic form before the spread is materially cheaper than chasing it across siblings later.

The canonical `crate-jmap-mail-client` does NOT have this code at all (no empty-creation-id validation on `email_set`), so there's no canonical-template comparison to make — the pattern was added in calendars-client without a sibling reference. Fix it here to set the right precedent for the future sibling sweep.

## Why not just leave it

It is plausibly leave-able. `ref m` works, the wire output is identical, the function is correct. The reason to flag it is workspace consistency: of the three sites in calendars-client (calendar.rs:96, event.rs:105, participant_identity.rs:79), all use `ref` — so the project has not yet decided this is OK. A drive-by contributor adding a fifth method that follows event_copy.rs:28 (which does NOT need `ref` because the function consumes `create` by value — `pub async fn calendar_event_copy(..., create: HashMap<...>)`, not Option) won't be sure which pattern to adopt.

## Fix shape

`if let Some(ref m) = create` → `if let Some(m) = &create` at three sites.

